<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Kastrioti</h1><p class="st-meta">1389 CE – 1444 CE (approx.) · capital Sinë</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Central Albanian lordship of the Kastrioti family, the base from which Gjergj Kastrioti, Skanderbeg, launched his revolt against the Ottomans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kastrioti family appears in the late 14th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pal-kastrioti">Pal Kastrioti</a> held two villages including Sinë, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diber">Dibër</a> region. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-kastrioti">Gjon Kastrioti</a> built a lordship stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mat-river">Mat</a> valley towards the Adriatic coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a>, accepting Ottoman overlordship after the 1380s while trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Ragusa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gjon ruled as a regional lord, balancing Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> with Venetian and Ragusan ties; he was made a citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and granted trading rights. As the price of submission he sent his sons to the Ottoman court as hostages, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Gjergj</a>, who was raised a Muslim and served the sultan as a commander.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Gjon's death in 1437 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> absorbed his lands. In 1443 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Gjergj</a>, now called Skanderbeg, deserted the Ottoman army after the battle of Niš, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Krujë</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. In 1444 he gathered the Albanian nobles in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a>, which inherited the Kastrioti lordship.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-kastrioti.jpg" alt="Principality of Kastrioti" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Arberian2444 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albania_Flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1389 CE – 1444 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sinë</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pal Kastrioti, Gjon Kastrioti, Skanderbeg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>, <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kastrioti lands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1416&amp;focus=principality-of-kastrioti" aria-label="Principality of Kastrioti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5163 698 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Kastrioti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5483 954L5709 970M5483 954L5478 945"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5478 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5483 954h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5483 954h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1416&amp;focus=principality-of-kastrioti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
